        </h2>
        <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-ink-muted dark:text-ink-muted">
          <p>
            A basic converter gives you files. A GIF to frames workspace gives you control over the frames you actually need. Instead of downloading every frame and cleaning up later, you can inspect timing, select a range, keep transparency, export metadata, and rebuild selected frames into a new GIF from the same screen.
          </p>
          <p>
            This matters most when a GIF contains dozens of frames, when the animation includes transparent regions, or when the final frames need to go into another tool like After Effects, CapCut, Unity, or a game engine. Working locally also means the source file never needs to leave your device.
          </p>
          <p>
